      "notes": {"design": "A straight Stairs tile fills its cell (a 7 m flight, 8 m long) and takes two levels, with its foot in the cell west of it and its head in the cell east: so the bay is three cells long and two floors high, and the hall beside the stair runs north of the cells' line (z 4 to 6.4 m), 3.6 m wide at the two ends where it meets a Dorm Bay's hall (z 2.8 to 5.2 m). North of the hall on each floor: a bathroom (toilets in stalls, basins), the office (an Oak Door with a glazed leaf standing open, a desk, bookcases) directly across from the stair, a bathroom (showers, basins). South: the stair hall at the foot (ground floor, west) and at the head (upstairs, east); the officer's quarters behind a shut diamond-panelled Oak Door under the stair's head on the ground floor and over its foot upstairs, seen through reinforced glass. A third floor goes over it as Dorm Bays, reached by the end stairs (a second straight flight cannot stand over the first: a Stairs tile claims both levels of its cell)."}
